      "id": "01",
      "title": "Partitions and ordering",
      "question": "How does Kafka scale a topic, and what order does it guarantee?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Kafka, question one. How does Kafka scale a single topic, and what ordering does it actually guarantee? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "How does a topic <em>scale</em>, and what <em>order</em> is guaranteed?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "A topic is split into partitions, spread across brokers, and each partition is an append-only log. A producer picks the partition by hashing the event's key. User A's login lands in partition zero, at offset zero. Other users spread over the other partitions, so the topic scales out. Every event for user A hashes the same way, so all of A's events land in one partition, in order. That is the guarantee: order within a partition only. Across partitions, there is no global order.",
