    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Consumer groups and offsets",
      "question": "How do consumers share a topic, and what happens when one crashes?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. How do several consumers share the work of one topic, and what happens when one of them crashes? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "How do consumers <em>share</em> a topic, and what if one <em>crashes</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "A consumer group divides a topic's partitions among its members: each partition goes to exactly one consumer. Adding a third consumer triggers a rebalance, and it takes over a partition. As they process, consumers commit their offsets: a bookmark per partition. If C2 crashes, the group rebalances again, and the survivors pick up its partitions, from the last committed offsets. Anything processed after that commit will be processed again. And a group can't use more consumers than partitions: the extras sit idle.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Partitions split across <em>a group</em>",
