    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Delivery semantics and replication",
      "question": "At-most-once, at-least-once, exactly-once: what decides which you get?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. At most once, at least once, or exactly once: what decides which delivery guarantee you get, and how does Kafka keep data safe? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "At-most, at-least, or <em>exactly</em> once: what decides it?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "It comes down to when you commit. Commit the offset before processing, and a crash loses the message: at most once. Process first, then commit, and a crash replays it: at least once, the default, so make processing idempotent. Exactly once needs an idempotent producer and transactions, that write results and offsets atomically. And for durability, each partition has a leader and followers; with acks set to all, a write is acknowledged only once every in-sync replica has it.",
          "props": {
            "title": "When you <em>commit</em> decides",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "before",
                "label": "Commit, then process",
                "title": "At most once",
                "body": "A crash loses the message.",
                "code": "may lose"
              },
              {
                "at": "replays",
                "label": "Process, then commit",
                "title": "At least once",
                "body": "A crash replays it: be idempotent.",
                "code": "default"
              },
              {
                "at": "transactions",
                "label": "Transactions",
                "title": "Exactly once",
                "body": "Idempotent producer; outputs + offsets atomic.",
                "code": "read-process-write"
              },
              {
                "at": "durability",
                "label": "Replication",
                "title": "acks=all",
                "body": "Leader + in-sync followers hold every write.",
                "code": "ISR"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. A topic scales by partitions, and a key always maps to the same one. Order holds only within a partition. A consumer group splits partitions, and resumes from committed offsets. And commit after processing for at least once, with idempotent handlers.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Kafka, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "scales",
                "text": "Topics scale by partitions; keys pick one.",
                "code": "hash(key) % N"
              },
              {
                "at": "Order",
                "text": "Order only within a partition.",
                "code": "per-key order"
              },
              {
                "at": "splits",
                "text": "Groups split partitions, resume from offsets.",
                "code": "rebalance"
              },
              {
                "at": "commit",
                "text": "Process, then commit; handlers idempotent.",
                "code": "at-least-once"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
